WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.947
A useful way to understand Transformers is to follow the data from beginning to end.

00:00:04.947 --> 00:00:07.457
We start with ordinary text.

00:00:07.457 --> 00:00:14.457
A tokenizer breaks the text into model-specific pieces and maps those pieces to numeric IDs.

00:00:14.457 --> 00:00:20.827
The model looks up an embedding vector for each ID and adds information about token position.

00:00:20.827 --> 00:00:28.092
Those vectors then enter attention layers, where each token can gather information from other tokens in the

00:00:28.092 --> 00:00:28.422
sequence.

00:00:28.422 --> 00:00:35.842
Feed-forward layers transform the updated vectors, and the process is repeated across many Transformer blocks.

00:00:35.842 --> 00:00:39.692
The final representations can support different outputs.

00:00:39.692 --> 00:00:45.332
An encoder may produce features for classification or named entity recognition.

00:00:45.332 --> 00:00:51.042
An embedding model may produce vectors for semantic search.

00:00:51.042 --> 00:00:56.862
A decoder may generate the next token repeatedly to create text.

00:00:56.862 --> 00:01:01.842
Whenever a later code example feels complicated, return to this path: text, tokens, IDs, vectors, attention,

00:01:01.842 --> 00:01:04.432
Transformer blocks, output.
